WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.450
- I wanna give a shout out
to our BAMF of the day,

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.840
the internet and YouTube's own Dr. Mike.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.020
- Passenger went into anaphylactic
shock during a flight.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.713
- Thankfully, Dr. Mike was on board.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.317
- I think it's story time.

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:19.317
(stirring music)
(heart monitor beeping)

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.130
I was headed to Israel to work

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.050
with a nonprofit called
America's Voices in Israel.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.540
They wanted me to meet some med tech CEOs,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.150
visit some hospitals, some nonprofits,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.850
visit with the university faculty

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.260
and chat about their research.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.680
But the trip going there was

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.370
quite eventful, to say the least.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.160
We're about two hours into the flight,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.780
we're over the Atlantic Ocean,

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.980
and the flight attendants
make that announcement.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.910
Is there a medical professional on board?

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.400
I don't know why I'm
holding a mic like this.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.233
They have phones.

00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:47.990
Is there a medical professional on board?

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:49.170
The flight attendant's right next to me.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.400
I'm a doctor, I can help.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.730
I'm not wearing shoes.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.060
I have a hoodie on.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.600
And I look like I haven't slept in days

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.050
because my strategy was to sleep

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.500
on the plane going there to
reset my circadian rhythm.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.490
The flight attendant does
believe me that I'm a physician,

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.150
brings me over, and I meet the passenger.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.320
He's a young guy, and he tells me

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.150
that he's starting to have
some swelling in his hands.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.320
And when I look at it,
I see hives are present.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.230
So, he's having an allergic reaction.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.560
And he said he's had an allergic reaction

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.550
the night prior, but it wasn't that bad.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.680
He took some Benadryl and it went away.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.500
Now, in that moment, I asked him if he had

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.410
the allergy medication
still on him, and he did.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:28.050
He had some steroid on him,
Prednisone, and Benadryl.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.380
I instructed him to take
another dose of both

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.570
and to let me know if anything at all

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.690
starts happening in his mouth or throat,

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.980
because that could be
a sign of anaphylaxis,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.340
which means that you have
swelling of the upper airways,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.820
which essentially, if left untreated,

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.260
can lead to death, and very, very quickly.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.700
Now, you might be
wondering, what is giving

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.700
Matt this serious anaphylactic reaction?

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.860
He didn't eat on the plane.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.870
My suspicion, based on talking with him

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.330
and hearing his history,
he was eating a significant

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.060
amount of red meat the night prior.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.170
Why is this interesting?

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.550
He normally doesn't eat red meat.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.860
He also says not too long
ago he was bitten by a tick.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.680
Now, if you're bitten by a specific tick

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.490
called the lone star tick,
which exists in the southernmost

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.640
region of the United
States but now is slowly

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.880
creeping its way up north, you can develop

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.720
an allergy to red meat,
specifically to alpha-gal.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.330
This is a sugar molecule that's found

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.450
in some mammals, but not people.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.310
And once you're bitten by this tick,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.290
if you eat an animal product
that has alpha-gal in it,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:30.600
you can have a delayed allergic
reaction much like Matt had.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.420
I right away started thinking
worst-case scenarios.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.350
What's gonna happen if
his throat starts swelling

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:36.970
and we're over the Atlantic Ocean?

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.840
Well, we can't land, so I would

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.650
have to administer epinephrine,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.390
which is essentially just adrenaline.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.860
And we give this medicine
into the upper thigh,

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.840
into the muscle, in order
to relax the airways,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.370
to tense up the blood
vessels throughout the body

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.200
so that we don't lose blood
pressure, which is one of the

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.500
really dangerous parts
of anaphylactic shock.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.430
If the epinephrine or
the EpiPen does not work,

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.800
the next step would be
to schedule a landing.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.090
And if the swelling
continues to get worse,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.310
I would be forced to make an incision

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.743
in this young man's throat.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.230
I started panicking.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.790
I haven't done this procedure
since medical school.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.640
I haven't seen it performed in years.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.390
I tried connecting to
the wifi to watch videos.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.730
I have no shame in saying this

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:21.830
because this is not a
procedure we do anymore.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.220
We intubate patients.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.700
But to do what we call a
cric, that's serious stuff.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.990
That's what you see on Grey's
Anatomy and House episodes

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.170
that we laugh at and say,
oh, no one ever does this.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.930
So, I was trying to
brainstorm what I can use

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:37.440
in terms of tools on the
airplane to make this incision.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.260
I was saying maybe a pen, maybe
a needle, maybe one of the

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.650
ampules that were found in the
kit that were made of glass,

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.250
and I could use a sharp piece of glass.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.210
My mind was racing.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.390
A few minutes later, the flight
attendant comes and gets me

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.470
and says, "Okay, he's starting
to have oral symptoms."

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.590
And that's when I kick into my

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.680
medical mode, full-on medical mode.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.560
I rush over, I look at his throat.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.870
It is definitely swelling.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.280
I can see it swelling.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.510
I just make the decision
to rush him to the front

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.760
of the plane, right by the cockpit,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.720
and it allows us to get some privacy,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.440
'cause I was gonna ask
him to take off his pants

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.970
in order to give him the injection.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.810
When I break open the kit that was found

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.810
on Delta's plane, I was shocked because

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.630
on the outside of the
kit, it said epinephrine.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:20.890
There were no EpiPens.

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:21.870
Now, you might be thinking, well,

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.650
if there's epinephrine,
what's the big deal?

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.120
You have epinephrine.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.030
It can be used just like an EpiPen.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:27.980
Well, yes and no.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:30.010
While it's the same active ingredient,

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.560
the dosages are different.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.710
Normally you give either .3
milligrams or .5 milligrams

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.660
during an anaphylactic reaction.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.090
With an EpiPen, that's
already pre-administered

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.690
and pre-filled with the
needle in it and everything.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.480
Now, what was in Delta's
kit was an epinephrine

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.580
injection kit for cardiac arrest.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.330
That's when someone's heart flat-lines.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:52.430
You're not shocking a patient.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.440
You're giving them this
dose of epinephrine.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:55.810
This is much higher dose.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.290
It's one milligram.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:00.280
And the needle is an inch and a half long.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:01.740
And I knew this was gonna be painful.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.170
So, here I am reading the instructions

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.270
on how to get the needle free,

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.990
because this isn't a kit
I was familiar with using.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.710
So, I was fumbling with it for
the first 30 seconds or so.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.920
That's why we need to
have an EpiPen on board.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.760
Because an EpiPen, had it been there,

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.830
all you have to do is read the

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.470
instructions really quickly,
boom, it auto-injects.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.303
It's simple.

00:05:20.303 --> 00:05:21.560
I told the patient what we're gonna do,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.820
I guess the passenger,
told the flight attendant,

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.160
and I injected it because his throat

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.110
was really starting to swell.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.380
He started screaming.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:29.780
I immediately withdraw the needle.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.810
I hand him an alcohol pad and I ask him

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.710
to hold pressure so I didn't
get any of his blood on me.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.040
He starts telling me that the area's

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.230
becoming very tender,
and I understand why.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:40.320
One of the main mechanisms of action

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.330
of how epinephrine works
is vasal constriction.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.270
It makes the arteries clamp down.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:46.510
That raises the blood pressure

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:48.030
and allows the person to live.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.670
But also, if you're giving it in this area

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.180
and it tightens all the blood vessels

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.840
up in this area, it causes a lot of pain.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.030
So, right away I explained
to him what was going on.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:59.980
I had him take a few steps
to walk around, got some ice

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:03.360
on his leg just to make
the pain a little bit less.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.840
We're checking his pulses,
checking his vitals.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.970
The reason why when you
administer epinephrine

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.760
or an EpiPen the first
step we do afterwards

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.200
is to get you to a
hospital is for monitoring,

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.230
to make sure that, A,
the allergic reaction

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.830
doesn't come back, B, to make
sure that your heart is okay

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:21.350
because I'm giving you adrenaline.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.910
That not only speeds up the heart,

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.300
raises the blood pressure.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.100
It can cause all sorts of side effects.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.860
So, now above the Atlantic
Ocean, the pilots are asking me,

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.310
the flight attendants are asking me,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:32.360
should we land in Canada?

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:33.400
Should we go back?

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.420
Should we land on the
islands near Portugal?

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.920
I ask the head flight
attendant, who was super sweet,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.780
to ask ground control
what their policy was.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.920
And what she told me Delta told her,

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.680
as long as a doctor was in
control of the situation

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:46.870
and felt the patient was stable,

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:48.640
no emergency diversion was needed.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.150
So, I checked his vitals a few

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.640
times after doing the injection.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:54.590
He was tachycardic as a
result of the epinephrine.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:56.090
His blood pressure was stable.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:57.320
It was in the normal range.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:58.340
It wasn't dropping.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.660
And we made the decision
to continue on route

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:04.410
to Tel Aviv, Israel, but
continually monitoring his status.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:05.640
I took out my iPhone.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.760
I was looking in his throat just

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.370
to make sure it wasn't swelling.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.180
Because he did scare me a couple of times.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:12.380
He couldn't tell if it was just

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.320
very dry or starting to swell.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:15.910
Whenever you get a dose of adrenaline,

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:18.290
I guess think about if
you're making a speech

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.550
and you're doing public speaking.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.150
You get nervous, you get dry mouth.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.740
That's the adrenaline kicking in there.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.290
So I thought that that's what likely

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.550
was going on because when I looked

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:27.820
with my iPhone, I did
not see any swelling.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.120
Everything looked okay.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:30.940
There was no need to
give a second injection.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.490
So, what my job was once
we gave the injection

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.470
was to, A, keep the patient calm,

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.100
and to check his vitals to make sure

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.770
there's nothing bad
changing, his blood pressure

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:42.340
wasn't dropping, his pulse
wasn't going too high,

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:45.000
and we could continue on with our flight.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.320
I did this pretty much every 30 minutes,

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.980
talked to him, stood over him.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.210
And at one point, he became really tired

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.810
because he took several doses

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.430
of Benadryl, Prednisone, adrenaline.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:56.550
He was getting sleepy 'cause he

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:57.980
didn't sleep well the night before.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.100
So, after watching him for
about four or five hours

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.220
after the injection, I felt comfortable

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.910
returning him to his seat,
telling his counselors

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.820
and people that he was on the trip with

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.270
about what was going on, that
if anything were to change

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:12.720
to right away come and get me.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.220
But my plan was to put him in his seat

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.620
for the last two hours
and every half hour or so

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.360
come on and check on him and
do another round of vitals.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.060
I did that up until the point of landing.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.170
His vitals were great.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.320
He was resting, his
throat wasn't swelling,

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.450
his pain in his leg was even resolving.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.100
So, we're able to land the plane

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.910
and he survived this transatlantic flight.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.390
The passengers around me thanked me,

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:36.520
but they didn't thank me for
saving this young man's life.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:38.140
I guess they didn't
know what was going on.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.770
They thanked me for not diverting

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.340
the plane and ruining their plans.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:43.550
I thought that was pretty funny.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.280
The crew on the plane were amazing.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:47.150
All the pilots, flight attendants,

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:49.130
they actually wrote me
a nice thank-you card

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.140
for the attention that I gave Matt.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.370
I was nervous, I was sleep-deprived,

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.650
but I was also very happy
that we had a good result.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.930
What makes this story kind of come

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.590
full circle is really interesting.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:01.500
There's a company called Taglit,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.870
which does birthright trips to Israel.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:05.870
And that's the trip that Matt was on.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.310
He was going to explore
Israel with his group.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.240
I actually was on the
same trip 10 years prior.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:13.340
And they said, well,
we have to connect them

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:15.700
somewhere where we meet on this trip.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:16.920
He was gonna be in Jerusalem.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.040
I was gonna be in Jerusalem.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.250
We had to have a meetup.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.250
And what do you know?

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.320
We made it happen.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.660
I was in a hospital in Jerusalem

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:27.610
doing some talks with one of
their university researchers

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:31.020
and touring their hospital,
and we found Matt there.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.290
He was already discharged,
but he came to visit.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.170
We're able to say hi, get a good laugh.

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.460
I was able to see that
he's perfectly fine.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.480
His leg is not bothering him too much,

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.880
he's touring all around
Israel doing hikes no problem.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.340
Now he's carrying an EpiPen around

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.500
to not repeat this experience.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.150
How is it possible that airlines

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.740
don't have EpiPens on board?

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.570
Allergies, anaphylactic attacks

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:56.050
are on the rise all throughout the world.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.120
We should be having
EpiPens available so that

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.590
the flight attendants could
be trained on how to use them,

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:03.700
and they can hopefully
save someone's life.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.500
I looked up if this has ever
happened before, and it has.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.000
Luckily in those situations,
another passenger

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.660
had an EpiPen on board, and
they were able to use theirs.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.330
But relying on that is not smart.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.010
Relying on the fact
that there's epinephrine

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:17.870
for cardiac arrest on the
plane is also not smart.

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:19.800
Easily in the haste of an emergency,

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.310
someone's gonna take that,
administer way too much

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:24.740
of a dose, and the person
can die as a result.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.060
My message to airlines?

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.700
Do what you have to do to
get an epinephrine pen,

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.470
specifically an
auto-injector pen, on board,

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.460
update it every year or 18
months so it doesn't expire,

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.000
and you're gonna save people's lives.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.640
I think this is just a no-brainer

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.710
that we absolutely have to accomplish.

